</ul><h1>Junior Builders</h1><p>“Thank you both for coming,” Val said, shutting the conference room door.</p>
<p>Greg and Jill mumbled a “sure” and an “of course” respectively, but their attention remained on the mass at the center of the table. Val had laid a sheet over the newest product to add a flourish to the unveiling.</p>
<p>“Ted messaged me that he’s running late,” Val said, “so let’s just get started. Without further ado, behold the newest addition to the Black &amp; Decker toy division!” With that, she whipped off the sheet. “It’s a buildable race car for children ages ten and up. It comes with the tools required for its construction and helps develop important STEM and problem-solving skills.”</p>
<p>“Oh, neat!” Jill said. “So it’s like an E–”</p>
<p>“Yes,” Val said, cutting her off, “a Constructor set.”</p>
<p>“No,” Jill said, “I was going to say an Erec–”</p>
<p>“We call it a Constructor,” Val said.</p>
<p>“But I see it too,” Greg said. “It reminds me a lot of an Erec–”</p>
<p>“<em>We</em> say Constructor,” Val said sharply.</p>
<p>“Okay, fine,” Greg said.</p>
<p>“Noted,” Jill said.</p>
<p>Just then, the door swung open, and Ted hobbled in. “I’m sorry I’m late. I’ve been having a rough morning.” His eyes locked on the race car at the center of the table. “Cool! Is that it? You know it kind of reminds me of an Erec–”</p>
<p>“Constructor,” Greg and Jill said in unison.</p>
<p>“Oh, is that what we’re calling it?” Ted slumped into a chair. “To differentiate our product from–”</p>
<p>“Don’t say it,” Val said.</p>
<p>“Um, okay,” Ted said. “And again, I just want to apologize for not getting here on time. My back has been killing me. I don’t know what it is.”</p>
<p>“If I may suggest something,” Jill said, “I think it’s your posture. You’re all bent over and slouching. What you want to do is sit upright and ere–”</p>
<p>“DON’T SAY IT!” Val said.</p>
<p>“Whoops, sounds like maybe I came at a tense time,” said Erica, leaning into the room.</p>
<p>“Just dealing with some marketing language mix-ups,” Val said. “What do you need, Erica?”</p>
<p>Erica stepped fully into the room. She held up her phone. “I was wondering if anyone could help me out. See, I know it’s not work, but I take my crossword streak very seriously, and I’m on the verge of solving my three-thousandth straight daily puzzle. But I’m stumped on three clues.”</p>
<p>“What are they?” Greg said.</p>
<p>Erica read from her screen. “Four-letters, ends with e, ‘Cleveland’s shores.’ Four letters again, starts with e, ‘Genuine to a German.’ And then two letters, starts with o, ‘contrasting conjunction.’”</p>
<p>“That’s simple,” Val said. “It’s Erie, echt, or.”</p>
<p>The room fell silent.</p>
<p>“Oh, one other thing, Erica,” Val said. “You’re fired.”</p>
<p>Erica sighed and wandered out of the room to gather her things.</p>
<p>“It’s kind of weird that she couldn’t get ‘or,’ right?” Jill whispered to Greg.</p>